  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nA few showers and strong thunderstorms may impact locations west\nof Billings early this morning. The primary impact with this\nconvection would be strong wind gusts to 60 mph. These storms have\na history of producing strong wind gusts near Butte and Helena.\n\nRidging and westerly flow will bring the return of very warm \nconditions today, with high temperatures in the 90s to low 100sF. \nModels and ensembles are in agreement of this warmth persisting \ninto next week. The warmest day of the stretch looks to be \nSaturday with highs up to 110F across southern Montana and \nnorthern Wyoming. Extreme Heat Warnings are in effect this morning\nthrough 9 PM MDT Sunday for most lower elevations of southern \nMontana and Sheridan County, Wyoming. For the foothills west and \nsouth of Billings, Heat Advisories are in effect this morning \nuntil 9 PM MDT Sunday. Along with the daytime temperatures will \ncome warm nighttime lows, limiting relief. Please take precautions\nto protect yourself from the heat.\n\nPrecipitable water values remain above normal today with high \nbased convection possible in the afternoon and evening. Minimum \nrelative humidity values will be very low, in the 10s percent \nacross the lower elevations. The primary impacts with any \nconvection will be gusty winds and brief moderate rainfall. Breezy\nconditions are also in store, with west winds gusting 20 to 30 \nmph.\n\nThe very warm, dry, and breezy conditions will bring elevated \nfire weather conditions into the weekend. Conditionally critical \nfire weather conditions are forecast on Saturday with the very hot\ntemperatures, low relative humidities, and high-based \nthunderstorms producing lightning and strong wind gusts. As a \nresult, a Fire Weather Watch is in effect Saturday afternoon \nthrough Saturday evening for portions of southern Montana, \nnorthern Wyoming and western South Dakota. The conditions exist on\nconvection producing the strong wind gusts and lightning strikes.\n\nA monsoonal wave comes through the ridge on Saturday, bringing \nthe mentioned chance for convection. Soundings show deep \ninverted-V profiles indicating the potential for strong wind \ngusts. PWAT values are still above normal, up to an inch in \nsouthern Montana, which could throw a wrinkle into high \ntemperatures with high clouds. Wet microburst activity cannot be \nruled out with steep lapse rates as well potentially mixing the \nhigher PWATs to the surface. While storms will not be dry, \nwidespread precipitation is out of the question. The latest HRRR \nshows a band of high- based convection moving west- east from the \nCrazies and producing wind gusts in excess of 50 mph through \nsouthern Montana Saturday evening. The latest Storm Prediction \nCenter Outlook has a Marginal Risk over portions of southern \nMontana for strong to severe wind gusts. Continue to monitor the \nforecast for model trends going into tomorrow.\n\nHot and dry conditions continue on Sunday, with a lower chance of\nstorms. Look for a weak wind shift to the north, especially in in\nsoutheast Montana where gusts in the 20s mph are forecast. Breezy\nconditions in the mountains are forecast as well with gusts of 20\nto 35 mph.\n\nSunday will also be hot and dry but with lesser thunderstorm \npotential, and a weak backdoor wind shift. North winds may be \nsomewhat breezy in the east. The forecast for much of next week \nwill be characterized by above normal temperatures and a daily \nrisk (10-25% chance) of diurnal thunderstorms. PWATs remain \nslightly wetter than normal so the storms will not be dry, but \nthey will be high-based and have the potential to produce \ngusty/erratic surface winds, which with the lightning itself will \nbe problematic for any new fires that develop over the coming \ndays.\n\nFinally for those keeping up on the July 25th drama. Billings \nairport has never (since 1934) reached 100F on this date, but it \nreally should happen this year. Current forecast is for 102F, \nconsistent with 700mb temps of +16C and deep mixing. With the \nwater vapor transport and eventual convection on Saturday, it is \nat least plausible that cloud cover holds back surface heating \njust a bit. The July 25th record is 98F, so it is possible to \nbreak the record and still not reach 100F. Stay tuned.\n\nPM/JKL",
